hey guys  hi uh thanks for taking the time today  um i wanted to start off by asking  one of the things uh i really like about  the documentary is it shows how sparks  forges their own path has you know kind  of they're always their own direction  but i was curious  what was your relationship like with the  record labels and companies that  may not have always been thrilled that  you weren't chasing trends  well we were really fortunate i think  the the one of the few advantages of  being at this stage of our career was  that  when we started the the record companies  were  a little bit more enlightened and that  they felt that  even if we weren't selling an enormous  amount of records  that that kind of what we were giving to  the label as a balance to  other artists was was a kind of  enriching what the label represented  that  we had the first two albums out in the  states that  that you know with todd runkin and  bearsville where  they didn't sell an enormous amount but  but warner brothers who would kind of  own  was a part of bearsville they they  really  appreciated what we could bring to to  the label even if it wasn't  selling a lot of records and then later  on we  we were lucky enough to be involved with  island records in the  70s where chris blackwell was really  an enlightened kind of person in the  visionary so  we're fortunate just that that the  things that have happened for us didn't  need to happen  within two or three days or else you're  gone  that there were people that would stick  with what we were doing  knowing that in time that there there  would be kind of  acceptance of it and i i don't think  that exists now and i  i feel bad for for bands now that are in  that  position where it's kind of do or die  within a very short amount of time  so many uh artists show up in this film  and they talk about  how much sparks meant to them and how  they were inspired by sparks and pulled  from sparks  four sparks i'm curious who are your  inspirations who are the artists that  you admire  well early on we we were huge  anglophiles  and so when we were growing up and it's  it's talked about  to a large extent in the documentary um  you know we we returned to the uk and  sitting here in l.a kind of going  against the grain of  of the laurel canyon kind of vibe that  was  also prevalent here at that time but we  always  loved the english bands that early on  like the early who the early kinks and  the  move and obviously the beetles and the  stones  yes we felt there was there was kind of  a certain  um bigger than life quality is how we  perceive  them that they they they look different  than us  they they acted different on stage it  was more of a show  that they were putting on at that at  that point and the  lyrics to some of those bands we  mentioned were kind of  really special and unique and seemed a  bit exotic  to us to being from la and hearing songs  about waterloo sunset and what's  waterloo  and and you know all of that village  greens  and tattoos you know the who's singing  of tattoos and like a  small a small kind of subject matter but  made into kind of a a bigger topic  because it's being addressed in a song  so all those things kind of intrigued us  and so we always kind of gravitated more  to the uk during the  the our more formative years of  of music how did you prepare  for directing your first documentary is  it a different tool set  than directing a narrative feature yeah  i guess the  the obvious difference is like you're  making a movie you're starting a story  from scratch  and like nobody knows what the story is  so you kind of like have the chance to  sort of  develop it as you go along but with this  is like you're taking like sort of  an existing story and figuring out how  to make it into a film  and so i mean if anything it was kind of  just there's like  an embarrassment of riches and i think  also because  part of my pitch to ronald russell was  that i wanted to tell the whole story  i didn't want to be like one of these  documentaries that you get well because  sparks are an unusual case because  they're a band that are sort of  bringing out music regularly by every  two or three years  and most other bands that started at the  same time have sort of either  become like a their own tribute act or  not releasing music or even when they do  it sort of they release it always feels  like kind of  pleasing the audience are writing pop  songs it's beneath them  so what sparks were doing to me was just  incredible and i felt like  it would be my thesis was basically to  say  like the 70s is not the golden period  the golden period is like 50 years long  like we're still in the golden period of  sparks so that was basically my thesis  and  and and so in terms of telling the story  it was about like  um looking at the archive obviously  and finding archives of things that we  knew existed but  like we had to kind of like get hold of  them and then it was  really i i thought i would create an  oral history because  obviously incredible to interview ronald  russell  and then you know but also to bring in  how they have touched other people's  lives  is to talk to fans famous  and non-famous you know and have like  that equilibrium so  there's there's no is a an equivalence  between  john taylor from duran duran and like  julia marcus who  stage invaded age 12 and sort of  what anybody has to say about sparks  whatever like experience level  or whatever knowledge is valid and so  that to me was important because it was  something to sort of show  the massive cultural footprint of sparks  then it needed to be a proper oral  history and in that sense i ended up  interviewing 80 people  and and that was that was amazing  because it just felt to me that that  the influence is so big that  you know just having three or four  interview interviews didn't seem quite  right  and also you know because you could show  it wasn't just like it was  one period that was influential it's  like five periods  and you can sort of see you know like  the sort of the fans that come into the  story throughout  you know i wasn't there at the start but  i came in at a certain point and and  never let go  and other people did the same do you  feel that this is a documentary you'll  have to keep updating i mean we have  annette coming out later this year and  i'm super excited for that or is this  sort of  do you feel like there's a good end  point for this story  this is the first 50 years and then  there's gonna be a part two and three so  it's  eventually it's gonna cover 150 years of  the sparks  it's going to be like my my collapse at  7up  [Laughter]  we've already we've signed on as part of  the contract too  so yeah it's a three picture deal three  picture  documentary deal the third one they  go into space with jeff basil  [Laughter]  so for you know someone looking to get  into sparks  this is a question for all three of you  would you recommend starting with the  documentary or do you think it would be  better to start  with a particular album or a particular  era  well i have this theory because i get  asked this a lot is what album should i  start with and the truth is  it's difficult to sum up sparks in one  album  because okay you could say commander my  house a classic album  but it's not the whole story so i sort  of sometimes got it down to like five i  mean  ultimately you know like there's more  than one great  best of and in fact there'll be a  soundtrack album for this film which is  a  pretty good like journey through the  decades as you could as you could want  but when i try and pin it down to  like five albums and and these can  change because there's so many there's  more than five great albums  like come on in my house number  next to my pants then i'm jumping  a lot already but um and hippopotamus  that's  that at least starts to get the sense of  the shape but obviously there's  even more than that so it is like that's  why i decided to  sort of document the entire discography  is i i'd heard from people who even were  aware of sparks that they said oh  i want to get into sparks i'm so daunted  by the discography so  it was great to do a documentary where  you could do an overview of the entire  career  and also it was important to do every  album because  a lot of music documentaries don't  they'll gloss over like  a flop like the bg's documentary which  was great i  really loved it but the only my only  criticism  was like oh i wanted to hear about the  sergeant pepper film  and obviously that thing gets skipped  over like skip over those two years  but with this it was like in a weird way  the the  the albums that maybe didn't sell well  at the time  they go on to be influential in their  own way so  eventually like no album is  more or less important than another one  thank you guys so much  i thought the documentary was fantastic  and just congratulations on everything  thank you thank you

